{"name":"Chanterelle Sauce","hex":"#a28776","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chanterelle","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A widely distributed edible mushroom, Cantharellus cibarius, being yellow and trumpet-shaped; or any similar mushroom of the genera Cantharellus, Polyozellus or Gomphus, not all of which are edible.","The highest string of the violin or similar instrument."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chanterelle"},{"term":"sauce","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A liquid (often thickened) condiment or accompaniment to food.","Tomato sauce (similar to US tomato ketchup), as in:"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To add sauce to; to season.","To cause to relish anything, as if with a sauce; to tickle or gratify, as the palate; to please; to stimulate."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sauce"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chanterelle","blocks":[{"text":"Chanterelle is the common name of several species of agaricomycetous fungi in the genera Cantharellus, Craterellus, Gomphus and Polyozellus. These fungi are orange, yellow or white, meaty and funnel-shaped. On the lower surface, most species have rounded, forked folds that run almost all the way down the stipe, which tapers down from the cap. Many species emit a fruity aroma and often have a mildly peppery taste."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/2007-07-14_Cantharellus_cibarius_Detail.jpg/330px-2007-07-14_Cantharellus_cibarius_Detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chanterelle","wikidata":"Q3942018"},{"title":"Sauce","blocks":[{"text":"In cooking, a sauce is a liquid, cream, or semi-solid food, served on or used in preparing other foods. Most sauces are not normally consumed by themselves; they add flavour, texture, and visual appeal to a dish. Sauce is a French word probably from the post-classical Latin salsa, derived from the classical salsus 'salted'. Possibly the oldest recorded European sauce is garum, the fish sauce used by the Ancient Romans, while doubanjiang, the Chinese soy bean paste, is mentioned in Rites of Zhou 20."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Lunch_sauces.jpg/330px-Lunch_sauces.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sauce","wikidata":"Q178359"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Chanterelle_on_sale.jpg/500px-Chanterelle_on_sale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Chanterelle_on_sale.jpg/1280px-Chanterelle_on_sale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Chanterelle_on_sale.jpg/500px-Chanterelle_on_sale.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chanterelles on sale in San Francisco","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChanterelle_on_sale.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/2010-06-18_Cantharellus_pallens_92488.jpg/500px-2010-06-18_Cantharellus_pallens_92488.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/2010-06-18_Cantharellus_pallens_92488.jpg/1280px-2010-06-18_Cantharellus_pallens_92488.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/2010-06-18_Cantharellus_pallens_92488.jpg/500px-2010-06-18_Cantharellus_pallens_92488.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Cantharellus pallens","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2010-06-18_Cantharellus_pallens_92488.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia